<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis IX of France</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1214–1270</span> · Also known as Saint Louis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis IX of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, king from 1226 to 1270, was revered as a model Christian ruler and canonised in 1297. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seventh-crusade">Seventh Crusade</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he was captured, then spent years refortifying crusader towns such as Caesarea. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1259">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1259 he settled the dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and he died besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighth-crusade">Eighth Crusade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france.jpg" alt="Louis IX of France" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saintlouis_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
